# The Three.js viewport is React Three Fiber

The campaign, host, and arena play `GameSession` in `web/src/engine/session/game_session.ts`.

Decided on 24 November 2026: the map and battle viewport port uses React Three Fiber (R3F). The HUD is already React, and declarative JSX is easier to reason about and refactor here than large imperative vanilla Three.js files.

Do not start a vanilla Three.js tree. If a piece of the port seems to need one, stop and ask first.

The viewport lives in `web/src/ui/viewport3d/`. `MapViewport3D` takes `MapViewProps`, mounts behind `?view=3d`, and loads on demand so Three.js stays out of the 2D bundle. The 2D map is the default until the 3D board draws unit sprites, drags, arrows, and the battle.

- Camera numbers stay in a ref and go straight to Three.js. Do not call `setState` inside `useFrame`.
- The canvas runs `frameloop="demand"`. Input calls `invalidate()`.
- Picking raycasts to the board plane and asks `regionPick.ts`. Colours come from `mapStyle.ts` and the faction palette.
- Pure parts (`boardCamera.ts`, `regionMeshes.ts`) have Vitest files under `web/tests/ui/viewport3d/`.
